#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58749cbe-7816-57fd-a7c2-054a48490984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406706} {:answer/id #uuid "5d64b8a8-da33-5a82-92ab-d47162348e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy-{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406707} {:answer/id #uuid "b90e506d-36bb-59dc-a9d8-181dd55c4171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+z{y}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406716} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d21bb7-6a0c-5042-8d17-4fbbdd6ece2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-xy+x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406718} {:answer/id #uuid "ea19559e-ae30-58d1-a09e-446a3ce435cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea19559e-ae30-58d1-a09e-446a3ce435cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406720} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46e9509-621a-57db-9c8d-caf61cc840c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a894d767-b55b-50b3-ac91-79c05f30a84d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124731" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y)\\,i+(2y+x+z)\\,j+(y-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+x+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2y+z)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2z & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff13513-2c62-551f-b51b-384eba917a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2y{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263152} {:answer/id #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263638} {:answer/id #uuid "63500018-57ff-5df1-bcb6-85380b2c7d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263864} {:answer/id #uuid "6c452c58-6b42-5c49-8585-0f39c63c429b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263939} {:answer/id #uuid "affded30-c6e0-5d42-b054-9af00e3bf815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263638} :answer-field/id #uuid "944f1edc-f0d0-51d2-983f-32b6ee33f7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74939473-4e44-5a23-a29d-cf0a790a9021" :question/math-academy-id "q-132080" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+2y)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dea30ea-6d79-5529-9cdd-2d23960e3a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406693} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfaa94c-d375-5358-a83c-08ec5ee1ffba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406695} {:answer/id #uuid "1e611253-3d99-509d-a228-1da84f8f823e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406696} {:answer/id #uuid "3f756955-ff37-5042-8335-ab5bb657b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406702} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3780f0-041a-52d5-a729-947e413446bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dea30ea-6d79-5529-9cdd-2d23960e3a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406693} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c4c17f-d3a1-54b7-85ad-332eeea0c340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98240cfd-67f4-53e3-a23f-45405606e2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132084" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea3ea19-4e92-5abd-88c9-1066a9bed13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697} {:answer/id #uuid "507422e5-32ae-5d72-ab98-c3033c109f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3y+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406705} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb42bf5-1fac-5e8e-8246-de98ef76481f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406708} {:answer/id #uuid "83195e93-81c4-5d1c-886b-c248874b3080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406710} {:answer/id #uuid "adcd9639-c75a-551b-9cba-97a7274611c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea3ea19-4e92-5abd-88c9-1066a9bed13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e00321-0365-5097-9e12-24d700cbbfb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6e8edea-70b0-524c-abbc-330734c9a85f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37343" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the functoin $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fb1154-e9d5-5abf-a426-4de833dd30db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406692} {:answer/id #uuid "30f1d918-8f71-5b48-b248-54d76cf6d20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406700} {:answer/id #uuid "3e98411d-fb1f-5835-98ed-95631d5c3272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406701} {:answer/id #uuid "92d03502-0368-5753-b847-438a0c827b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406711} {:answer/id #uuid "e73fdb1a-1b58-5eba-ad8a-569d465a93c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fb1154-e9d5-5abf-a426-4de833dd30db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406692} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a04f87b-0a35-5b54-b59b-82f029326452" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3be742e-e07b-5a79-9e12-1ef7df6cb24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81736" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03292879-7e19-58c5-bf30-9a82b7757e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406691} {:answer/id #uuid "10ef096b-3da6-5469-9aaf-056abb9c9b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406694} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5e2bd6-42c5-529f-9f08-cbd53403ea48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406699} {:answer/id #uuid "b1384745-a3d2-57a2-83cf-2248f12bae46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406714} {:answer/id #uuid "c18a0f77-fbcd-5ceb-b30c-ca0b3feee4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03292879-7e19-58c5-bf30-9a82b7757e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406691} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b902c9-1d56-5de9-9587-969c0111a4bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7faa34ab-beed-5e97-be56-8c5b15402d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81739" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cca9c71-9e8d-570c-8946-ced4f1d50328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348703} {:answer/id #uuid "2a68e94a-4173-5ae7-858e-76a0731e97f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348827} {:answer/id #uuid "58860516-bdb9-5c40-81cc-99fbd0564951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=(x+y){e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349265} {:answer/id #uuid "5cca20bd-b007-5611-a5cf-cd625413e2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349306} {:answer/id #uuid "d106b7ad-431a-5269-9f98-9101b7fb0c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a68e94a-4173-5ae7-858e-76a0731e97f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348827} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7a6d5b1-1736-5e32-bd6e-3958724c6738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e91b9e21-1500-52f9-9378-332978dc9aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81741" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(z) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z) \\\\ h(z) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c1bf68-0a65-5287-b32f-c0e4dc3478f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+xz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348644} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1acbc8-d106-5b62-8d36-a7cb3a78a863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+yz+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350134} {:answer/id #uuid "d83bed69-94c4-50e6-be9a-7dcda2208ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350347} {:answer/id #uuid "f516143d-39f1-5b86-9e6a-3fe23efc5edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xy+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350596} {:answer/id #uuid "fda21360-2a77-5e5c-bdf6-2c33643d2238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda21360-2a77-5e5c-bdf6-2c33643d2238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350684} :answer-field/id #uuid "b222692b-cb3d-5740-9bf6-1564da255bac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfdbf33d-2f2f-5cc3-b43d-2537f87d3a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81752" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=(yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,i+({e}^{xz}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{xz}+x)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz{e}^{xz}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =y{e}^{xz}+xz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={e}^{xz}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z) & =zy{e}^{xz}+x \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd49044-48d7-5753-8938-92019f630b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+2y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406698} {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a1c5-e1f1-531f-8432-aafd884d17cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+2yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406703} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2af6d4-f404-59ca-99d2-24985eb2eafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406709} {:answer/id #uuid "a809662b-6dd9-5c73-b6ef-ca6fafbec01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406712} {:answer/id #uuid "b668a6c8-35b7-56c2-aa49-6197e1a607d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f2af6d4-f404-59ca-99d2-24985eb2eafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406709} :answer-field/id #uuid "419ffafb-e705-5c7d-80bf-60c8356135f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "327918f1-3cba-54fe-8e7b-c743d7b1d3cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-81755" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2x{y}^{3}z\\,i+(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2})\\,j+({x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3}z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x{y}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int {z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z) & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"}]}
